window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][33]={"university_physics_en:M33-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M33-L1 – Atomic Fine Structure and Angular-Momentum Coupling<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M33 – Master: Atomic, Molecular and Optical Physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M12: quantum numbers and angular\nmomenta; M23: atomic spectroscopy; M32-L4: line shapes.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compute L·S eigenvalues for a p\nelectron with spin 1/2 and understand the limits of that model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Why do apparently degenerate atomic levels generate multiple spectral\nlines? An electron’s orbital angular momentum couples to its spin. In a\nsimple one-electron model, quantum numbers l, s, and j label the total\nangular momentum J=L+S. Different allowed values of j then acquire\ndifferent energies even at fixed n and l.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>A simple effective spin–orbit Hamiltonian is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑳<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑺<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_{\\mathrm{so}}=\\lambda\\,\\mathbf L\\cdot\\mathbf S/\\hbar^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑱<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑳<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑺<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf J^2=(\\mathbf L+\\mathbf S)^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nit follows that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑳<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑺<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑱<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑳<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑺<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf L\\cdot\\mathbf S=[\\mathbf J^2-\\mathbf L^2-\\mathbf S^2]/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOn\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>l<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|l,s,j,m_j\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nits dimensionless eigenvalue is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>l<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>l<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[j(j+1)-l(l+1)-s(s+1)]/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe size and sign of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndepend on the atom and level; it is an effective energy parameter\nhere.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>l<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>l<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta E_j=\\frac{\\lambda}{2}\\left[j(j+1)-l(l+1)-s(s+1)\\right].<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For a p electron,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">l=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j\\in\\{1/2,3/2\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTaking\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=2\\,\\mathrm{meV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta E_{3/2}=+1\\,\\mathrm{meV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta E_{1/2}=-2\\,\\mathrm{meV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na modeled separation of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm{meV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure sketches these shifted levels; it is not a scale drawing for\na specific element.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_272_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"p-electron levels before and after simplified spin–orbit coupling, with j equal to one half and three halves.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">p-electron levels before and after\nsimplified spin–orbit coupling, with j equal to one half and three\nhalves.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Effective spin–orbit splitting for l = 1 and s = 1/2 with\npositive λ.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which values of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>j<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare possible for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">l=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta E_{1/2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>l<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">l=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=4\\,\\mathrm{meV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why should the sketch not be transferred directly to complex\nmultielectron atoms?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j=3/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta E_{1/2}=-\\lambda=-4\\,\\mathrm{meV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Electron correlation, alternative coupling regimes, and\nfield-dependent interactions can matter.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>A constant λ replaces a level-dependent microscopic interaction in\nthis teaching model. Hyperfine structure, Zeeman effects, relativistic\ncorrections beyond the model, and line-strength selection rules are\nomitted. NIST supports the quantum-number conventions, not the\nfabricated parameter.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Atomic energy structure determines the optical frequencies to which a\ntransition responds. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M33&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://physics.nist.gov/Pubs/AtSpec/node03.html\">NIST – Atomic Spectroscopy: Atomic States, Shells and Configurations<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Quantum numbers, angular-momentum coupling and spin–orbit fine structure; selected NIST definitions; Subject relevance to lesson: M33-L1 – Atomic Fine Structure and Angular-Momentum Coupling · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-421-atomic-and-optical-physics-i-spring-2014/pages/readings/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Atomic and Optical Physics I: Readings<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Atomic-physics bibliography; does not verify individual formulas; Subject relevance to lesson: M33-L1 – Atomic Fine Structure and Angular-Momentum Coupling · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M33-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M33-L2 – Two-Level Atoms and Coherent Rabi Oscillations<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M33 – Master: Atomic, Molecular and Optical Physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M12: quantum time evolution; M26:\ndensity matrix; M33-L1.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Find a resonant π-pulse duration\nfrom the Rabi frequency and distinguish coherent dynamics from\ndecoherence.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>An atom driven between two selected states by a narrow optical field\ncan often be approximated as a two-level system. Under coherent resonant\nexcitation, the excited-state population oscillates periodically.\nMeasured fluorescence also depends on decay out of the excited\nstate.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>In a rotating frame and under the rotating-wave approximation, the\ncoherent drive may be written\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H/\\hbar=(\\Delta\\sigma_z+\\Omega\\sigma_x)/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the angular-frequency detuning and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe resonant Rabi angular frequency. Starting in the ground state, on\nresonance with no dissipation one obtains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_e(t)=\\sin^2(\\Omega t/2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA π pulse satisfies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Omega t_\\pi=\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand ideally inverts the population. Decay and dephasing require optical\nBloch equations with relaxation terms.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>π<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_e(t)=\\sin^2\\!\\left(\\frac{\\Omega t}{2}\\right),\\qquad t_\\pi=\\frac{\\pi}{\\Omega}\\quad(\\Delta=0).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Omega/(2\\pi)=1.0\\,\\mathrm{MHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_\\pi=0.50\\,\\mathrm{\\mu s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.25\\,\\mathrm{\\mu s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe ideal excited-state population is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_e=1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe graphic follows\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nover two cycles. Note that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis an angular frequency, so the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nconversion matters.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_301_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Sinusoidal-squared excited-state population with marked π and 2π pulses.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Sinusoidal-squared excited-state\npopulation with marked π and 2π pulses.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Undamped resonant Rabi driving: a π pulse inverts the\npopulation.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the π-pulse duration for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Omega/(2\\pi)=2\\,\\mathrm{MHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What ideal population follows a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npulse?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What physical processes damp Rabi oscillations?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_\\pi=0.25\\,\\mathrm{\\mu s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_e=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif the system started in its ground state.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Spontaneous emission, collisions, drive fluctuations, and phase\nnoise.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The sine-squared law assumes resonance, coherent driving, a valid\nrotating-wave approximation, and negligible decay during the pulse. It\nis not a general solution for strongly open systems. MIT provides the\ncourse framework for optical Bloch equations; the numerical example is\nconstructed independently.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>After the driven atom, the next topic is molecular potential energy\nand vibration. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M33&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-422-atomic-and-optical-physics-ii-spring-2013/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Atomic and Optical Physics II: Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Course lecture index on quantum optics, Bloch equations, cooling, and metrology; selected topics; Subject relevance to lesson: M33-L2 – Two-Level Atoms and Coherent Rabi Oscillations · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-422-atomic-and-optical-physics-ii-spring-2013/pages/syllabus/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Atomic and Optical Physics II: Syllabus<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Syllabus on open quantum systems, optical interactions, and ultracold atoms; Subject relevance to lesson: M33-L2 – Two-Level Atoms and Coherent Rabi Oscillations · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M33-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M33-L3 – Molecular Potentials, Morse Oscillator and Franck–Condon Factors<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M33 – Master: Atomic, Molecular and Optical Physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M12: potential wells and harmonic\noscillators; M33-L1; basic molecular bonding.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Account consistently for\nelectronic energy and nuclear repulsion and quantify Morse-oscillator\nanharmonicity.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>In molecules, heavy nuclei usually move more slowly than electrons.\nThe Born–Oppenheimer approximation first solves the electronic problem\nat fixed nuclear positions. If the electronic Hamiltonian excludes\ninternuclear repulsion\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{nn}(R)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe nuclear potential curve is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi>e<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U(R)=E_{el}(R)+V_{nn}(R)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nif it includes that term, it must not be counted twice. Nuclear motion\ntakes place on this potential surface while nonadiabatic couplings\nremain small.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>A bound diatomic molecule can be approximated by a Morse potential\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U(R)=D_e[1-e^{-a(R-R_e)}]^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith zero at its minimum.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes dissociation energy from that minimum and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe equilibrium separation. Levels measured from the minimum are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ℏ<\/mi><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>ℏ<\/mi><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_v=\\hbar\\omega_e(v+1/2)-[\\hbar\\omega_e]^2(v+1/2)^2/(4D_e)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhile bound states exist. Adjacent level spacings decrease with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor vibronic transitions, the Franck–Condon factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><msubsup><mi>χ<\/mi><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>e<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msubsup><mi>χ<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_{v\\prime v}=|\\langle\\chi^{(e)}_{v\\prime}|\\chi^{(g)}_v\\rangle|^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribes vibrational overlap alone; without electronic transition\ndipoles, populations, and selection rules it is not a complete line\nintensity.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ℏ<\/mi><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ℏ<\/mi><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_v=\\hbar\\omega_e\\left(v+\\frac12\\right)-\\frac{(\\hbar\\omega_e)^2}{4D_e}\\left(v+\\frac12\\right)^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For a constructed example take\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℏ<\/mi><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hbar\\omega_e=0.20\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_e=5\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe dimensionless anharmonicity is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ℏ<\/mi><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.01<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_e=\\hbar\\omega_e/(4D_e)=0.01<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>→<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=0\\rightarrow1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntransition,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ℏ<\/mi><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.196<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_1-E_0=\\hbar\\omega_e(1-2x_e)=0.196\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA harmonic oscillator would predict\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.200<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.200\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure sketches the potential and a few increasingly close\nlevels.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_029_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Molecular Morse potential with an asymptotic dissociation limit and representative bound vibrational levels.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Molecular Morse potential with an\nasymptotic dissociation limit and representative bound vibrational\nlevels.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Anharmonic molecular vibration: level spacing decreases with\nexcitation.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℏ<\/mi><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hbar\\omega_e=0.10\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_e=2.5\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_1-E_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith those values?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">When should\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{nn}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe added separately to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi>e<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{el}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.01<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_e=0.01<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.10<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.02<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.098<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.10(1-0.02)=0.098\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Only when internuclear repulsion is not already included in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi>e<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{el}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The Morse model is useful for an individual bond coordinate, not\ngeneral polyatomic motion or crossings between electronic surfaces. The\nexact Morse-level expression applies only to the finite set of bound\nstates. Translating Franck–Condon overlaps into intensity estimates\nfurther assumes an approximately coordinate-independent electronic\ntransition dipole (Condon approximation).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Molecular spectroscopy relates states to optical transitions. The\nnext lesson quantizes the light field itself. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M33&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.physchem.no/physchem_book/chapters-17-born-oppenheimer.html\">Physical Chemistry II – The Born–Oppenheimer Approximation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Clamped-nuclei approximation, nuclear-repulsion term and Morse levels; Subject relevance to lesson: M33-L3 – Molecular Potentials, Morse Oscillator and Franck–Condon Factors · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.pci.tu-bs.de/aggericke/PC4e_osv/Spectroscopy050119/node23.html\">TU Braunschweig – Franck–Condon Principle<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Franck–Condon factors as vibrational overlaps; not complete intensity predictions; Subject relevance to lesson: M33-L3 – Molecular Potentials, Morse Oscillator and Franck–Condon Factors · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M33-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M33-L4 – Photon Statistics: Fock States, Coherent Light and Correlations<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M33 – Master: Atomic, Molecular and Optical Physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M13: probability distributions; M26:\ndensity operator; M33-L2.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate Poisson photon-number\nprobabilities and distinguish g(2)(0) for Fock and coherent states.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>A single quantized optical mode has discrete photon-number states\n|n⟩. A coherent state |α⟩ is a superposition of number states with a\nPoisson distribution and mean photon number n̄=|α|². Intensity\nfluctuations can distinguish coherent light, ideal single-photon light,\nand thermal light.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For a coherent state the probability of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nphotons is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><\/msup><msup><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>n<\/mi><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mi>!<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_n=e^{-\\bar n}\\bar n^n/n!<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nConsequently the mean and variance both equal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe normalized zero-delay second-order correlation for one mode is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g^{(2)}(0)=\\langle\\hat n(\\hat n-1)\\rangle/\\langle\\hat n\\rangle^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt equals one for ideal coherent states. For a Fock state\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|N\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nit is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-1/N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence zero for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|1\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe normalization is undefined for vacuum with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle n\\rangle=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><\/msup><mfrac><msup><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>n<\/mi><\/msup><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mi>!<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_n=e^{-\\bar n}\\frac{\\bar n^n}{n!},\\qquad g^{(2)}(0)=\\frac{\\langle\\hat n(\\hat n-1)\\rangle}{\\langle\\hat n\\rangle^2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar n=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.0183<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_0=e^{-4}\\approx0.0183<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.0733<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_1=4e^{-4}\\approx0.0733<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe photon-number standard deviation equals\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>4<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt4=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe graphic compares the Poisson distribution to an ideal single-photon\nstate fixed at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt depicts quantum-state statistics, not detector efficiency.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_043_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Bars of the Poisson photon-number distribution with mean four, alongside an ideal single-photon distribution.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Bars of the Poisson photon-number\ndistribution with mean four, alongside an ideal single-photon\ndistribution.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Coherent light has Poisson photon statistics; an ideal\nsingle-photon state has fixed photon number.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>n<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar n=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g^{(2)}(0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the ideal Fock state\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|2\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g^{(2)}(0)=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot alone imply a perfect laser experiment?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.368<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-1}\\approx0.368<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-1/2=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Real measurements depend on modes, background, time resolution, and\nlosses; the number does not specify the entire field.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>These expressions assume a well-defined ideal single mode and quantum\nstate. Multimode fields, losses, detector noise, and time binning modify\nmeasured correlations. The Cornell reference is a lecture index; a\nseparate quantum-optics chapter supports the formulas.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Photons carry momentum during absorption and emission, enabling\ncooling of slow atoms. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M33&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://mochizuki-tus.github.io/QM_Textbook_EN/ch60_photons_phonons.html\">Quantum Mechanics – Field Quantization and Photon Statistics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Coherent-state Poisson distribution and g(2)(0) for Fock states; Subject relevance to lesson: M33-L4 – Photon Statistics: Fock States, Coherent Light and Correlations · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://courses.cit.cornell.edu/ece531/Lectures/Lectures.htm\">Cornell – ECE Quantum Optics Lecture Index<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lecture index on photon statistics and quantum coherence; general orientation; Subject relevance to lesson: M33-L4 – Photon Statistics: Fock States, Coherent Light and Correlations · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M33-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M33-L5 – Laser Cooling: Doppler Force and Temperature Limit<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M33 – Master: Atomic, Molecular and Optical Physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M1: momentum and energy; M33-L2:\ntwo-level system; M33-L4: photons.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate the Doppler\ntemperature from the natural linewidth under explicit model\nassumptions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>In Doppler cooling, red-detuned beams are arranged so that a moving\natom preferentially absorbs photons counterpropagating to its motion.\nAveraged over many events, photon recoil slows the atom. Spontaneous\nemission simultaneously produces random recoil and momentum diffusion.\nThe balance between friction and diffusion creates a limiting\ntemperature in the simplest model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For a closed two-level transition with spontaneous decay rate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(angular-frequency linewidth in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{s}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>),\nweak saturation, and optimal red detuning\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta=-\\Gamma/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe elementary Doppler model gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_D=\\hbar\\Gamma/(2k_B)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the angular-frequency linewidth; a quoted linewidth\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Gamma/(2\\pi)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin hertz must be multiplied by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbefore substitution. This is not an absolute bound on all cooling\nmethods.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_D=\\frac{\\hbar\\Gamma}{2k_B}\\qquad\\left(\\Delta=-\\frac{\\Gamma}{2}\\right).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For a modeled transition with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Gamma/(2\\pi)=6.0\\,\\mathrm{MHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>6.0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Gamma=2\\pi\\cdot6.0\\cdot10^6\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.054571817<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>34<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hbar=1.054571817\\cdot10^{-34}\\,\\mathrm{J\\,s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.380649<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>23<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_B=1.380649\\cdot10^{-23}\\,\\mathrm{J/K}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.44<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>144<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_D\\approx1.44\\cdot10^{-4}\\,\\mathrm K=144\\,\\mathrm{\\mu K}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram sketches counterpropagating beams and the viscous force.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_145_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Counterpropagating red-detuned laser beams, a moving atom, opposing photon momentum, and a schematic damping force.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Counterpropagating red-detuned laser\nbeams, a moving atom, opposing photon momentum, and a schematic damping\nforce.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Doppler cooling balances velocity-dependent scattering against\nrecoil diffusion.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the sign of the cooling-beam detuning?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is it possible to cool below\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It doubles in the same model.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta&lt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(red detuning).<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Additional internal structure and polarization gradients enable\nother mechanisms; further stages can also follow.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The equation assumes low intensity, an effectively closed two-level\ntransition, and appropriate opposing beams. Multilevel atoms,\nsub-Doppler processes, finite recoil energy, and magneto-optical traps\nrequire richer models. The linewidth here is a hypothetical example, not\na measured species parameter.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Cooled atoms can be confined in spatial traps for controlled studies.\n<a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M33&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-422-atomic-and-optical-physics-ii-spring-2013/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Atomic and Optical Physics II<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Laser forces, cooling and ultracold atoms; does not prove all Doppler-model assumptions; Subject relevance to lesson: M33-L5 – Laser Cooling: Doppler Force and Temperature Limit · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://opg.optica.org/abstract.cfm?uri=iqec-1988-PD1\">Optica – Observation of Atoms Laser-Cooled Below the Doppler Limit<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Doppler cooling bound for a basic two-level model and existence of sub-Doppler processes; Subject relevance to lesson: M33-L5 – Laser Cooling: Doppler Force and Temperature Limit · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M33-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M33-L6 – Atomic Traps and Quantized Motion in a Harmonic Potential<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M33 – Master: Atomic, Molecular and Optical Physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M12: harmonic oscillator; M33-L5:\ncooling; M31: model limits.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compute vibrational energies and\nthe ground-state spatial width of a harmonic atom trap.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Optical dipole traps, magnetic traps, and ion traps confine particles\nin spatial potentials. Near a stable minimum, a second-order Taylor\nexpansion often suffices. Along one coordinate this produces a harmonic\noscillator. Its quantized motion differs from a perfectly stationary\nclassical atom: even the ground state has finite spatial spread.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≃<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(x)\\simeq V_0+\\tfrac12m\\omega^2(x-x_0)^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe energies are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_n-V_0=\\hbar\\omega(n+1/2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the ground-state spatial standard deviation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_x=\\sqrt{\\hbar/(2m\\omega)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the trap minimum, and the quadratic approximation applies near that\nminimum. Greater particle mass or stronger confinement (larger\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ω<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\nreduces the quantum spatial spread.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mfrac><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/msqrt><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_x=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\hbar}{2m\\omega}},\\qquad E_n-V_0=\\hbar\\omega\\left(n+\\frac12\\right).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>Consider a hypothetical atom of mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.44<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>25<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=1.44\\cdot10^{-25}\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega/(2\\pi)=1.0\\,\\mathrm{kHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.241<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_x=\\sqrt{\\hbar/(2m\\cdot2\\pi\\cdot1000)}\\approx0.241\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe energy spacing is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta E=h\\cdot1.0\\,\\mathrm{kHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram sketches a parabolic trap and a qualitative ground-state\nprobability density.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_124_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Harmonic trapping potential with quantized energy levels and a Gaussian ground-state probability density.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Harmonic trapping potential with\nquantized energy levels and a Gaussian ground-state probability\ndensity.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Quantization of trapped motion leaves a finite spatial spread\neven in the ground state.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange if the trapping frequency is quadrupled?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(E_0-V_0)/(\\hbar\\omega)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why might the harmonic model fail far above the bottom of the\ntrap?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The spatial width is halved.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(E_0-V_0)/(\\hbar\\omega)=1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Higher-order potential terms, finite trap depth, and couplings\nbecome important.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Not every trap is harmonic; coupled three-dimensional potentials\nrequire normal modes and principal axes. A classical thermal ensemble\ndoes not have the same spatial distribution as the quantum ground state.\nThe numbers are fabricated, not an experimental trap prescription.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Optical fields can confine atoms and generate new frequencies in\nnonlinear media. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M33&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1002/qute.202000022\">Quantum Science and Technology – Optical Dipole Trapping in a Parabolic Mirror<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Harmonic-trap approximation and ground-state spatial spread; selected formula; Subject relevance to lesson: M33-L6 – Atomic Traps and Quantized Motion in a Harmonic Potential · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-422-atomic-and-optical-physics-ii-spring-2013/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Atomic and Optical Physics II: Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Course lecture index on quantum optics, Bloch equations, cooling, and metrology; selected topics; Subject relevance to lesson: M33-L6 – Atomic Traps and Quantized Motion in a Harmonic Potential · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M33-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M33-L7 – Nonlinear Optics: Second-Harmonic Generation and Phase Matching<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M33 – Master: Atomic, Molecular and Optical Physics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M15: wave optics and dispersion;\nM32-L4: spectroscopy; M33-L4.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Evaluate how second-harmonic\noutput scales with pump intensity, crystal length, and phase\nmismatch.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>In a noncentrosymmetric medium, the induced electric polarization can\ncontain a term quadratic in the driving electric field. An incident\nfield at frequency ω therefore produces a polarization component at 2ω\nand can generate second-harmonic light. Nonlinearity alone is\ninsufficient: waves accumulating different phases may cancel partly\nalong the crystal.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>ϵ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msup><mi>χ<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>E<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>χ<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><msup><mi>E<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>⋯<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=\\epsilon_0[\\chi^{(1)}E+\\chi^{(2)}E^2+\\cdots]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe squared sinusoidal field contains a doubled-frequency component as\nwell as a DC contribution. With constant pump intensity, a plane wave,\nan undepleted fundamental field, and negligible absorption,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>∝<\/mo><msubsup><mi>d<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><msubsup><mi>I<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><msup><mi>L<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sinc<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_{2\\omega}\\propto d_{\\mathrm{eff}}^2I_\\omega^2L^2\\operatorname{sinc}^2(\\Delta kL/2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sinc<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{sinc}(x)=\\sin(x)/x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta k=k_{2\\omega}-2k_\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe proportionality constant includes refractive indices and frequency\nfactors. Only at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta k=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoes the full\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>L<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nscaling persist without phase loss.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>∝<\/mo><msubsup><mi>I<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><msup><mi>L<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sinc<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_{2\\omega}\\propto I_\\omega^2L^2\\operatorname{sinc}^2\\!\\left(\\frac{\\Delta kL}{2}\\right).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>At perfect phase matching, doubling\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives a factor of four in the undepleted-pump limit; doubling\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalso gives a factor of four. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta kL=\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe normalized phase-matching factor is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sinc<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>π<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.405<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{sinc}^2(\\pi/2)=4/\\pi^2\\approx0.405<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe graphic shows the main peak at zero mismatch and the decaying side\nlobes.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_259_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Normalized squared-sinc phase-matching curve with a central maximum and side lobes.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Normalized squared-sinc phase-matching\ncurve with a central maximum and side lobes.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>In the undepleted-pump limit, phase mismatch reduces\nsecond-harmonic output through a sinc² factor.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_{2\\omega}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis halved, all else fixed?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sinc<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{sinc}^2(0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which real effect limits indefinite\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>L<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngrowth at high conversion?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It falls to one quarter.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nby the continuous limit.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Pump depletion; absorption and group-velocity walk-off can also\nlimit conversion.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The displayed scaling is not a universal design rule for focused,\nultrashort, or strongly converted pulses. Phase matching depends on\ndirection, polarization, dispersion, temperature, and possibly periodic\npoling. The cited paper supports the scaling and sinc² factor; it is not\na complete tensor derivation or experimental sign-off.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>This module connects atomic fine structure, coherent driving,\nmolecular vibration, quantum light, cooling, trapping, and frequency\nconversion.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11239842/\">Scientific Data – Second-Harmonic Generation Tensors<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Quadratic pump-intensity and length scaling with a sinc² phase-matching envelope; Subject relevance to lesson: M33-L7 – Nonlinear Optics: Second-Harmonic Generation and Phase Matching · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-422-atomic-and-optical-physics-ii-spring-2013/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Atomic and Optical Physics II: Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Course lecture index on quantum optics, Bloch equations, cooling, and metrology; selected topics; Subject relevance to lesson: M33-L7 – Nonlinear Optics: Second-Harmonic Generation and Phase Matching · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
